THOUGHT: The NullAway error is because `position` is annotated @Nullable, so `position.n` is an unsafe dereference. The iterator contract says `next()` should only be called when `hasNext()` is true, but NullAway doesn’t assume that. A safe fix is to guard the dereference: if `position` is null, throw `NoSuchElementException` (standard for Java iterators). This avoids new errors and keeps behavior correct. I’ll modify the file accordingly and then run the Gradle compile to ensure the NullAway error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/libgdx && sed -i '154,158c\ \ \ \ \ \ @Nullable
\ \ \ \ \ \ @Initializer
\ \ \ \ \ \ @Override
\ \ \ \ \ \ public Node<E> next() {
\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ if (position == null) throw new java.util.NoSuchElementException();
\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ previousPosition = position;
\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ position = position.n;
\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ return previousPosition;
\ \ \ \ \ \ }
' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/utils/SortedIntList.java && ./gradlew gdx:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -E "NullAway: .*errors" new_build_output.log || tail -n 40 new_build_output.log```

